<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chamunda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chamundi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chamunda is a fierce form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mother goddess Devi, one of the seven or eight Matrikas. In the Devi Mahatmya she is created from the goddess's anger and slays the demons Chanda and Munda, from whom she takes her name. She is shown as gaunt and terrifying, and is worshipped at many hill shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including Chamundi Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and the hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chamunda.jpg" alt="Chamunda" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Hindu_Goddess_Chamunda_LACMA_M.80.3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
